atio":0.8,"watchRatio":0.49670000000000003},{"ratio":0.81,"watchRatio":0.4927},{"ratio":0.82,"watchRatio":0.489},{"ratio":0.83,"watchRatio":0.4866},{"ratio":0.84,"watchRatio":0.48450000000000004},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.4818},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.48009999999999997},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47450000000000003},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4693},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.4661},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.4646},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.4583},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4531},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.4472},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.4457},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.4391},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.4322},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.42369999999999997},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.4114},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.3969},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3908}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":132,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"Bengaluru's most iconic building exists because a Russian delegation insulted India in 1951. A Soviet cultural group toured the city that year. They made one observation that cut deep. Every grand building they saw was British. Where was the Indian architecture? They asked it plainly. The remark reached Chief Minister Hanumanthaiah. He took it personally. He vowed to [music] build something that would dwarf every colonial structure in the city. Not a copy, not a tribute, a monument to a free democratic India. He chose pure Bangalore granite. He hired over 5,000 workers. The result became Vidhana Soudha. [music] And then he added the line carved across the front entrance. In English and Kannada, sits one sentence. Government's work is God's work. One Russian remark built South India's grandest seat of power."}}